I shall relate in full the account of that Manu Vaivasvata,
for your benefit, if you wish to hear it here;
this great foundation is established in the Purāṇa.

The sages said:
Recite in detail, Lomaharṣaṇa, the birth of Pṛthu,
and how this earth was milked by that great-souled one;

The Earth said:
“I will do all this, O hero, there is no doubt;
but look out a calf for me, through which I, full of love, may flow.

Lomaharṣaṇa said:
The details cannot be told in even hundreds of years, O twice-born;
hear about all the manvantaras in brief, O twice-born.

Lomaharṣaṇa said:
Sagara had two wives whose sins had been burned by asceticism;
the elder, the daughter of the king of Vidarbha, was named Keśinī.

Lomaharṣaṇa said:
Long ago the sages asked my teacher this very question;
I shall tell you that which you ask, O best of the twice-born.

The sages said:
O blessed one, we wish to hear of the land of action on the surface of the earth;
you ought to tell us, O lord of gods, of the rarely attained holy place of liberation.

The sages said:
Since it was said by you that action nowhere else yields merit
or leads to evil, O best of the gods, except in Bhārata;

Brahmā said:
Listen, O twice-born, to the realm of Bhārata in its ninefold division;
they should be understood as separated by the ocean, yet equal to one another:

The sages said:
The sun god has been declared by you to be without qualities and eternal;
yet we have heard from your words that he was born again in twelve ways.

Brahmā said:
Then the lord Vibhāvasu emerged from that splendour;
the sun was seen then by her, resembling molten copper, the lord.

Brahmā said:
Having spoken thus, the radiant lord disappeared;
and having attained all that she desired, she ceased her austerities.

The king of mountains said:
“Lord, I do not disregard brāhmaṇas, who are gods on earth;
but hear what was previously resolved, O high-minded one!

The goddess said:
“O lord of alligators, tell me quickly what is not condemned by the virtuous;
that is done, there is no doubt about this, because brāhmaṇas are dear to me.”

auspicious with freshly sprouted mushrooms, plantain shoots,
and tender leaves emerging on creepers, trees, and the like,
and made resonant by groups of languid frogs awakened by the loving touch of auspicious showers of water;

by means of the delightful peacock cries that in an instant
bring about the shattering of pride even in spirited, passionate women
whose minds are haughty with disdain toward their lovers;

When his merit is exhausted, having returned here into an eminent family of yogins,
he becomes a brāhmaṇa knowing the four Vedas, who has crossed to the further shore of the Vedas and auxiliary sciences;
resorting to Vaiṣṇava yoga, he then attains liberation.

This greatness of Indradyumna has been related by me;
all that is wondrous, the secret Puruṣottama, has been declared,
ancient, supremely esoteric, auspicious, and delivering from cyclical existence.

At the festival of the gods, the Unborn [Brahmā], upon looking at his mother's toe,
incurred the state of being fallen through sin;
the compassionate Father gave him the holy Gaṅgā, stationed in a meritorious water-pot, which purifies through mere remembrance.

Brahmā said:
Saying 'So be it', the hosts of gods departed for heaven;
and the Blessed One, the lord of the gods, entered the womb of Aditi.

Brahmā said:
As he was praising him thus, the one whose banner bears a bull,
glorious, accompanied by Umā and surrounded by Gaṇeśa and the attendants, appeared before him;

The Lord said:
You have asked for the benefit of the three worlds;
now ask, free from all fear, for a boon for your own benefit!

Yājñavalkya said:
“The lord of waters is your guru, your father-in-law, and one who acts for your good;
go to him and ask, O king; he will instruct you in what is beneficial.”

Brahmā said:
Hearing the word 'Ilā', that king was overwhelmed with rage;
rebuking the yakṣiṇī, he asked her again about the doe.

Brahmā said:
Hari dispatched his discus to slay the lord of spirits;
the lord of spirits swallowed that discus even as it came rushing.

Brahmā said:
Then the Blessed One, bearer of the conch, discus, and mace, was pleased;
he said to them: "For what purpose have you all come? I shall do that."

Brahmā said:
Then, when the gods had gone, Viṣṇu set out for the sake of the discus;
going to the Godāvarī, he commenced worship of Śambhu.

Brahmā said:
The Avighnatīrtha is proclaimed as the destroyer of all obstacles;
I shall recount the event that happened there too; listen with devotion, O Nārada.

Who delighted his mother with charming dances and songs,
and with all desires and playful amusements;
to that Śrī Gaṇeśa, then exceedingly pleased, I take refuge.

Brahmā said:
There is also that which is called Somatīrtha, spoken of by the great-souled ones;
by bathing and giving gifts there, one obtains the fruit of drinking soma.

By them, without doubt, comes the alleviation of all diseases;
from them alone comes food, the preservation of all living beings.
Here the medicinal plants, revered by the world, devoid of ego, spoke to me.

Brahmā said:
The gods and the ṛṣis accepted the word, and Soma did as well;
and the medicinal plants also [accepted] that word, and then went to their own abode.

I cannot bear life, fortune, or high station;
I am wasted away seeing the sons born of Aditi.
I fall into an intolerable state, distressed,
beholding Aditi's supreme prosperity.
Even entering a forest fire surely brings happiness;
not even in a dream should a rival's splendor be witnessed.

Śambhu said:
"O sage, do not harbor anger against the husband of Śacī;
attain peace, O very wise one, for the Maruts have become immortals."

For all beings desiring fruits in this world and the next,
the attainment of a virtuous son is always cherished;
therefore, grant your favor with respect to bathing here."

Brahmā said:
From that time on, that holy place was called Putratīrtha;
there, through bathing, giving, and the like, one obtains all desires.

The female owl said:
Terrified of you, people run after [dharma];
terrified of you, they practice celibacy;
terrified of you, the resolute act virtuously;
terrified of you, they become devoted to ritual acts.

Terrified of you, they undertake fasting to death;
terrified of you, they wander from the village to the forest;
terrified of you, they adopt gentleness;
terrified of you, they partake of soma-drinking;
terrified of you, they devote themselves to gifts of food and cattle;
terrified of you, they proclaim discourse on Brahman.

Brahmā said:
Both gods spoke to the two birds renowned in the world,
for the attainment of dharma and fame, desiring the welfare of the worlds:

fortunate, intelligent, heroic, and causing sons and grandsons to flourish.
By the offering of rice-balls and the like there, the ancestors attain release;
from sin born of mind, speech, and body, a person becomes freed by bathing.

Śambhu said:
“From my seed and through you, Suvarṇa of immense power was born.
All prosperities completely abide in this Suvarṇa;

Indra said:
My enemy was powerful, and like Saturn to behold;
by him I was bound, brought down low, and repeatedly humiliated.

Even while residing in heaven, Indra constantly follows that Vṛṣākapi.
Seeing him attached to another, Śacī became angry with love;
comforting her, Śatamanyu said this with a laugh:

Brahmā said:
The confluence of Vāṇī is renowned, where Hara is the Lord of Speech;
that tīrtha frees from all sins and grants all desires.

Having cut down this doubt, a creature attains the highest desired object.
Desire enters like a female goblin and burns away all happiness;
cutting it down with the sword of perfect 'I'-ness, one attains liberation while living.

There he saw the thousand-eyed Indra, surrounded by auspicious gods,
praised by the Siddhas and Sādhyas, watching excellent dancing,
listening to sweet song, and fanned by Apsarases;

The Fire said:
In the beginning, [she is] a wife (patnī); from being maintained (bharaṇa), she is a bhāryā;
from giving birth (janana), she is a jāyā; by her own virtues, she is a spouse (kalatra).
O blessed one, you bear forms such as these;
carry out the word that has been spoken by me.

Brahmā said:
There is another holy place, the Yāyāta, where Śiva is Kālañjara;
it quiets all sins, and its history is told by me.

Having performed a most sinful deed, I have reached a terrible rebirth;
and having been cooked in the hells, I have again reached the intermediate state.
Whatever persons commit evil deeds, such is their destination!”

Ever since, this has been well known as destroying piśāca-hood and great disease;
and men's great sins quickly go to destruction
by the mere remembrance of it.

O best of rivers, you will be accompanied by all tīrthas;
whatever tīrthas there are in heaven, earth, and the underworld,
they will go to bathe in you, O Mother, when I am in Leo.'

With Śiva seated here, Nandin constantly [wanders] on the bank of the Gaṅgā;
he is dharma itself walking; therefore it is remembered as Nandītaṭa.
It is also the Ānanda tīrtha, because it increases all joy.'

Brahmā said:
Thus asked, Dharmarāja related everything truly;
he made known without remainder the course of all those deeds.

Harṣaṇa said:
“My father is Tvaṣṭṛ’s son, terrifying, and my mother is Viṣṭi, terrifying;
and my brothers are high-spirited. By what means may they become peaceful in mind,

Brahmā said:
Having heard Dharma's words, Harṣaṇa went to the Gautamī;
being pure, he praised Hari, the lord of gods, and Hari was pleased.

Brahmā said:
Patatritīrtha is declared as destroying disease and destroying sin;
by merely hearing of it, a man becomes one who has accomplished his purpose.

overcome with sorrow, that Aruṇa spoke to the Sun;
seeing them fallen to the earth, Aruṇa said this to the Sun:
‘Revive these two, O hot-rayed one, before they die!’”

Madhucchandas said:
"Your father sits bound in the world of the gods, O noble-minded one;
through gambling and many vices, he has fallen from his kingdom.

Brahmā said:
The Sāmudra tīrtha has been declared to bestow the fruits of all tīrthas;
I shall describe its true nature; listen attentively, Nārada.

The cowherds said:
"O Rāma, O Kṛṣṇa! This region of land is always guarded by Dhenuka;
therefore indeed these [fruits] have been left alone.

Homage to you who hold the mace in hand, O Viṣṇu; homage to you who hold the conch in hand!
I see this form of yours adorned with tangible emblems;
I do not know that supreme [form] of yours; be gracious, O Supreme Lord!

Vyāsa said:
Then, having bowed, delighted, he returned from home to Śambhu;
then, seeing his banner broken, rejoiced, he experienced supreme joy.

The Blessed Lord said:
May this Bāṇa, to whom a boon was given by you, live, O Śaṅkara;
out of respect for your words, this disc has been turned back by me.

The gods said:
"O Lord, by this boon that Asura will oppress us;
therefore, be gracious, O Lord, and devise his destruction as well.

Vāsudeva said:
"Cast off fear, O immortals! I grant you freedom from fear;
likewise, O gods, you will regain heaven without delay.
